    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d392">

                <pb facs="1160" n="1154" sameAs="frus:frus1894app2/pg1154"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Comly.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 105.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-07-27"><hi rendition="#italic">July 27,
                        1881</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch, marked “separate and confidential,” of the 4th instant, touching the
                    reply of the Hawaiian Government to her Britannic Majesty’s commissioner in the
                    matter of the British claims.</p>
                <p>Adverting to that part of your dispatch suggesting “that the Hawaiian Government
                    would be glad to have an intimation of the opinion of the Secretary of State”
                    upon the subject, I desire to refer you to my instruction No. 102, of the 30th
                    ultimo, in which I have distinctly stated that the claim of Great Britain for
                    the same privileged treatment by Hawaii as that enjoyed by the United States
                    pursuant to its reciprocity treaty was wholly inadmissible.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">James G. Braine.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
